  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0052 database is designed to manage various aspects of a discussion forum, including user management, thread organization, and notification tracking. The database consists of 11 tables: failed_jobs, likes, migrations, notifications, password_resets, replies, subscriptions, taggables, tags, threads, and users. The failed_jobs table stores information about failed job attempts, including the connection, queue, payload, exception, and timestamp of failure. The likes table tracks user likes on threads and replies, linking users to the liked content through the likeable_id and likeable_type fields, which reference the id and type of the liked item in the replies and threads tables, respectively. The migrations table keeps track of database schema changes, with each entry containing a migration name and batch number. The notifications table stores notifications sent to users, including the notification type, notifiable type and id, data, read status, and timestamps. The password_resets table stores password reset tokens for users, linked to their email addresses. The replies table contains individual replies to threads, with each reply linked to its author through the author_id field, which references the users table. The subscriptions table tracks user subscriptions to threads and other content, linking users to the subscribed content through the subscriptionable_id and subscriptionable_type fields, which reference the id and type of the subscribed item in the threads and other tables. The taggables table links tags to threads and other content, with each taggable item linked to a tag through the tag_id field, which references the tags table. The tags table stores tag names and slugs. The threads table represents individual discussion threads, with each thread linked to its author through the author_id field, which references the users table, and to its solution reply through the solution_reply_id field, which references the replies table. The users table stores user information, including their GitHub ID, username, email, name, and other details, with each user linked to their replies and threads through the author_id field.",
  "gold_question": "Community Portal for Laravel\n\n 1) Background / Context\nThe project involves developing a community portal powered by Laravel.io. This platform functions as a forum for Laravel enthusiasts to facilitate problem-solving, knowledge sharing, and community building. It serves as a hub for discussions, idea sharing, and assistance on Laravel-related topics.\n\n 2) Objectives\n- Provide a seamless forum experience for users to discuss Laravel-related issues.\n- Enable knowledge sharing and community engagement among Laravel enthusiasts.\n- Facilitate user interactions through features like threads, replies, and tags.\n\n 3) Stakeholders & Users\n- Laravel Enthusiasts: Primary users who participate in discussions and knowledge sharing.\n- Administrators: Manage the platform, oversee user interactions, and ensure smooth operation.\n\n 4) Functional Descriptions\n- User Registration: Users can sign up using their email or GitHub credentials.\n- Threads: Users can create threads to initiate discussions or queries.\n- Replies: Users can post replies to threads or as responses to other replies.\n- Likes: Users can like threads and replies to show appreciation or agreement.\n- Notifications: Users receive notifications related to interactions on their posts or threads they follow.\n- Tags: Threads can be tagged for categorization and improved searchability.\n- Subscriptions: Users can subscribe to threads or topics to receive updates.\n- Password Resets: Users can reset their passwords using a secure token-based system.\n\n 5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Scalability: The system should efficiently handle a growing number of users and posts.\n- Security: Secure authentication and authorization, with provisions for banning users when necessary.\n- Performance: The system should ensure responsive interactions and fast loading times.\n- Reliability: Provide consistent uptime and handle failed job processes effectively.\n- Usability: User-friendly interfaces for ease of use, even for those not technically proficient.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0052.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0052\nCREATE TABLE `failed_jobs` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `connection` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `queue` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `payload` text NOT NULL,\n  `exception` text NOT NULL,\n  `failed_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `likes` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `likeable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `likeable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `likes_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `migrations` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `migration` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `batch` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `notifications` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `notifiable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `notifiable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `data` text NOT NULL,\n  `read_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `password_resets` (\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`email`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `replies` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `thread_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `author_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `replies_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`author_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `replies_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`thread_id`) REFERENCES `threads` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `subscriptions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subscriptionable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subscriptionable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `subscriptions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `taggables` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `tag_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `taggable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `taggable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `taggables_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`tag_id`) REFERENCES `tags` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tags` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `threads` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `author_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `solution_reply_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `threads_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`author_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `threads_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`solution_reply_id`) REFERENCES `replies` (`id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `github_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
